A67. His nibs. ~ NOBS
A68. The dumb boy didn't know this was loaded. ~ LADEN
A70. Where the Ptolemies got all washed up. ~ NILE
A71. A bawl club. ~ GLEE
A72. Small shop keepers don't like to carry this sort of person. ~ OBESE
A73. They find it easy to make indolence out of this in England. ~ DOLE

D1. Schooner crossings. ~ BARS
D2. Make free with the help. ~ ABET
D3. What doctors do to patients after the cure. ~ BLEED
D4. Prevailing shades of Ma Eve's fall frocks. ~ YELLOWS
D5. Rugged British writer (init.). ~ TH
D6. A seaside boom. ~ ROAR
D7. Where Swiss yodelers get the air. ~ ALP
D8. What a book thief always leaves. ~ CLEW
D9. Baseball's greatest. ~ TY
D10. This group reports sound business (plur.). ~ TALKIES
D11. A wet and dry storm. ~ SLEET
D12. Even ministers can't do this without swearing. ~ AVER
D13. Stretches in the Capone manner. ~ RAPS
D18. This kind of gin is a liar. ~ SLOE
D20. Clinker. ~ SLAG
D22. Too many bachelors of these are wedded to them. ~ ARTS
D24. Invariable complaint lodged against poor Yorick. ~ ALAS
D27. Sun-tanned queen who got up to meet old Sol. ~ SHEBA
D28. This made the silkworm turn to idleness. ~ RAYON
D30. This makes you hot when it's cold. ~ STARE
D31. A lot of people are keeping a watch on this joint. ~ WRIST
D32. A place to park Fanny. ~ DIVAN
D33. How Little Willie took your watch. ~ APART
D35. This is how Telemachus began to make his name. ~ TEL
